<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title><%= vuln.name %> - Vuln-World</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 0; background: #f9fafb; color: #374151; line-height: 1.6; }
    header { background: #1f2937; color: #fff; padding: 15px 30px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }
    header a { color: #9ca3af; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold; }
    header a:hover { color: white; }
    .container { max-width: 900px; margin: 40px auto; background: white; padding: 40px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); }
    h1 { margin-top: 0; color: #111827; }
    h2 { color: #1f2937; border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb; padding-bottom: 10px; margin-top: 40px; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 18px; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
    .badge { display: inline-block; padding: 4px 8px; background: #e5e7eb; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold; margin-right: 10px; color: #4b5563; }
    .btn { display: inline-block; padding: 12px 24px; background: #2563eb; color: white; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; font-weight: bold; margin: 20px 0; }
    .btn:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }
    pre { background: #1f2937; color: #f8f8f2; padding: 20px; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; }
    .vuln-code { border-left: 4px solid #ef4444; }
    .sec-code { border-left: 4px solid #10b981; }
    .diagram { background: #f3f4f6; padding: 20px; font-family: monospace; white-space: pre; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; }
    ul { padding-left: 20px; }
    li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <a href="/vulnerabilities">← Back to Vulnerabilities</a>
  </header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1><%= vuln.name %></h1>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.category %></span>
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.difficulty %></span>
    </div>
    
    <a href="/labs/<%= vuln.lab %>?challenge=<%= vuln.id %>" class="btn">Start Lab (<%= vuln.lab %>)</a>
    
    <h2>What Is It?</h2>
    <p>Insecure File Upload vulnerabilities occur when a web server allows users to upload files without sufficiently validating the file's name, type, contents, or size. If the uploaded file can be executed by the server as a script, this leads to Remote Code Execution (RCE).</p>

    <h2>In Simple Words</h2>
    <p>Imagine a secure mailroom that only accepts postcards. One day, they stop checking what's inside the envelopes and someone mails a bomb. File upload vulnerabilities happen when the server expects a harmless image but blindly accepts and runs a malicious script.</p>

    <h2>Where Does It Appear?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Profile picture uploads</li>
      <li>Document or attachment features</li>
      <li>CSV data imports</li>
      <li>Content management systems (CMS) media libraries</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Does It Occur?</h2>
    <div class="diagram">
Attacker uploads "shell.php" pretending it's a profile picture.
    |
[Web Server saves "shell.php" to /public/uploads/shell.php]
    |
Attacker visits http://site.com/uploads/shell.php
    |
[Web Server executes shell.php, granting the attacker control]
    </div>

    <h2>HTTP Example</h2>
    <pre>
POST /upload HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----WebKitFormBoundary

------WebKitFormBoundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="shell.php"
Content-Type: application/x-php

&lt;?php system($_GET['cmd']); ?&gt;
------WebKitFormBoundary--
    </pre>

    <h2>Burp Suite Investigation</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Upload a harmless file (like `.jpg`) and intercept the request in Burp Suite.</li>
      <li>Change the filename in the request from `image.jpg` to `shell.php`.</li>
      <li>If blocked, try bypassing filters (e.g., `shell.php.jpg`, `shell.PHP`, `shell.phtml`).</li>
      <li>Find where the file is stored and navigate to it in the browser to see if it executes.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How Developers Accidentally Create It</h2>
    <p>Vulnerable Code:</p>
    <pre class="vuln-code">
app.post('/upload', (req, res) => {
  const file = req.files.profilePic;
  // Saving the file directly with its original, unchecked name
  file.mv('/var/www/html/uploads/' + file.name, (err) => {
    res.send('Uploaded successfully!');
  });
});
    </pre>
    <p>Secure Code:</p>
    <pre class="sec-code">
app.post('/upload', (req, res) => {
  const file = req.files.profilePic;
  const ext = path.extname(file.name).toLowerCase();
  
  // Validate extension against strict allowlist
  if (!['.jpg', '.png'].includes(ext)) {
    return res.status(403).send('Invalid file type');
  }
  
  // Generate a random, safe filename
  const safeFilename = crypto.randomUUID() + ext;
  file.mv('/var/www/uploads/' + safeFilename, (err) => {
    res.send('Uploaded successfully!');
  });
});
    </pre>

    <h2>Impact</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Remote Code Execution (RCE) and full server takeover.</li>
      <li>Defacement of the website by overwriting legitimate files.</li>
      <li>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) if HTML/SVG files are uploaded and served.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Find It in Real Applications</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Test different executable extensions for the server environment (e.g., `.php`, `.jsp`, `.asp`, `.py`).</li>
      <li>Try double extensions (`file.php.jpg`) or null byte injection (`file.php%00.jpg`).</li>
      <li>Check if uploading `.svg` files allows Stored XSS.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Remediation</h2>
    <p>Never trust the user-provided filename or Content-Type header. Always enforce a strict allowlist of file extensions and validate the actual file contents (magic bytes). Store uploaded files in a directory that does not have execution permissions, or ideally, store them on an external storage service (like AWS S3) rather than the local filesystem.</p>

    <h2>Secure Design Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use a strict extension allowlist.</li>
      <li>Generate a random filename server-side (do not use the original filename).</li>
      <li>Serve files with `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and disable execution in the upload folder.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Lab Investigation Guide</h2>
    <p>In this lab, there's a feature to upload a new profile picture. Investigate what happens when you upload a normal image versus a file with an executable extension like `.php` or `.js`. Can you upload a file that shouldn't be allowed?</p>

    <h2>Post-Lab Explanation</h2>
    <p>The application accepted your uploaded file without verifying its extension. It only saved the file using the original name you provided. By uploading a file with a dangerous extension like `.php`, you proved that an attacker could upload a web shell and achieve Remote Code Execution on the server.</p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
